# Pages this archive draws by hand on purpose. checkpages prints every line of
# this file on every run, so an exemption nobody can justify is an exemption
# somebody will notice.

namesakes: not a list of namesakes. This page exists to record a DECISION NOT TO HAVE ONE — Falco is the Italian for falcon and became a surname independently in many places, so a famous Falco is no more likely to be kin than a famous Smith. Defranceski's page collects notable people of a rare name and Booyzen's is about repeated FORENAMES. One page name, three different arguments.
research-log: an INDEX OF FILES, not a log. One markdown working file per register volume, with its byte count and a link into the repository — the notes themselves are the thing, and this page is their table of contents. Booyzen's and Luwinski's research logs are chronological narratives of what was done; Defranceski's is a tracker of open questions.
dna: a side-by-side COMPARISON of two people across the groups they share, not a distribution — plus a generation-eight horizon argument that exists to say what autosomal DNA cannot see. Defranceski's bars are one distribution per person. Same word, different chart.
who/[slug]: a page per NAME over RECORDS, and its own header explains why it cannot be a page per person: the roster's 10,432 rows carry 4,820 distinct names, and «Pasquale Falco» alone names seventy-nine records across sixty years and is certainly several men. The three archives whose who/[slug] uses the kit's Namesakes are listing PEOPLE. This one refuses to, on purpose, and a component that presents a name as a person would undo the refusal.
